<h2>What to expect</h2>
<p>A typical filling visit takes 30–60 minutes, depending on size and location. The tooth is numbed, the decay is cleaned out, and a tooth-colored composite resin is shaped and bonded into place. Most patients can eat normally right away.</p>

<h2>Composite vs. older silver fillings</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Composite bonds directly to the tooth, so we can preserve more healthy structure.</li>
  <li>It's tooth-colored and essentially invisible.</li>
  <li>It doesn't expand and contract with temperature the way amalgam does, reducing the risk of long-term cracking.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Replacing old fillings</h2>
<p>Old amalgam fillings don't need to be replaced just for cosmetic reasons. But if a silver filling is cracked, leaking, or the tooth around it is breaking down, that's the right time to switch to a modern composite restoration — or, if the damage is significant, a crown.</p>

<h2>Preventing future fillings</h2>
<p>The best filling is the one you never need. Regular cleanings, twice-daily brushing, and daily flossing remain the most effective cavity-prevention tools. We can also recommend custom fluoride trays or sealants if you're cavity-prone.</p>
